          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the case comparison, Patient A and Patient B had identical CBC values but different diagnoses. Which single lab finding most reliably distinguished iron deficiency anaemia from the other condition?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mean cell volume of 70 fL</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both patients had the same low MCV. A low MCV indicates microcytosis but does not distinguish between its causes — iron deficiency and thalassaemia trait both produce microcytic cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haemoglobin of 9.5 g/dL</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both patients shared the same haemoglobin level. The degree of anaemia alone cannot separate iron deficiency from thalassaemia trait or other microcytic causes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum ferritin level</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Patient B's ferritin was 4 (depleted stores = IDA), while Patient A's was 68 (normal stores). Ferritin reflects total body iron stores and is the key test to confirm whether microcytic anaemia is due to true iron deficiency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Peripheral blood smear morphology</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While the smear can offer clues (pencil cells in IDA, target cells in thalassaemia), both conditions produce a hypochromic microcytic picture. Ferritin is the more definitive discriminator.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the case: both patients shared the same Hb, MCV, and smear appearance. The distinguishing finding was ferritin — low in Patient B (true IDA) and normal in Patient A (not iron deficient)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Serum ferritin is the most reliable single test to confirm depleted iron stores and distinguish IDA from other microcytic anaemias with normal stores."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
