          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which laboratory parameter is the earliest to become abnormal when the body begi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which laboratory parameter is the earliest to become abnormal when the body begins to run low on iron?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haemoglobin concentration</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haemoglobin falls only after both storage and transport pools are exhausted (stage 3). It is a late marker of iron deficiency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum iron</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Serum iron reflects the transport pool, which is depleted in stage 2. The storage pool, measured by ferritin, is drawn down first.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mean cell volume</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MCV falls only when iron deficiency is severe enough to impair haemoglobin synthesis (stage 3). By then, stores and transport are already depleted.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum ferritin</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Ferritin reflects total body iron stores. When iron balance turns negative, the body draws down storage iron first, so serum ferritin drops before serum iron or haemoglobin change — making it the earliest warning sign of iron deficiency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the sequence: the body draws down storage iron first (ferritin falls), then transport iron drops (serum iron falls, TIBC rises), and only last does haemoglobin fall. Ferritin is the earliest change."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Serum ferritin is the earliest marker because storage iron is the first pool depleted. A low ferritin can signal iron deficiency even when the blood count is still completely normal."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
