          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 58-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with iron deficiency anaemia. She has no history of dietary restriction or malabsorption. Which etiological category should be investigated first?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Decreased dietary intake</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While poor intake can contribute, in postmenopausal women chronic blood loss must be excluded first — it is the most dangerous cause to miss, particularly because it may indicate an underlying malignancy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Increased physiological demand</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Increased demand applies to pregnancy, lactation, infancy, and adolescent growth spurts. A 58-year-old postmenopausal woman does not fall into any of these categories.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Decreased iron absorption</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Malabsorption (coeliac disease, gastrectomy, atrophic gastritis) is possible but was already excluded in the history. Chronic blood loss remains the priority to rule out.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Increased loss through chronic bleeding</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In postmenopausal women and older men, iron deficiency anaemia is gastrointestinal bleeding until proven otherwise. The priority is to exclude colorectal malignancy and other sources of occult GI blood loss.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Recall the principle: patient demographics point to the most likely cause. In postmenopausal women, chronic GI blood loss is the most important category to investigate first, particularly to rule out malignancy."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right. The video emphasises: in older men and postmenopausal women, think GI bleeding and rule out malignancy first. Demographics guide which etiological bucket to open."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
