          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A student uses the TAILS mnemonic to list causes of microcytic anaemia. Which of</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A student uses the TAILS mnemonic to list causes of microcytic anaemia. Which of the following is the only condition in the differential that involves genuinely depleted total body iron stores?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Thalassaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In thalassaemia, the defect is in globin chain synthesis, not iron depletion. Iron stores are normal or even increased — giving iron supplements is unnecessary and potentially harmful.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anaemia of chronic disease</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In ACD, total body iron stores are normal or high — iron is trapped in macrophages by hepcidin. The marrow is iron-starved, but the body's total iron is not depleted. This is functional, not absolute, deficiency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Iron deficiency anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. IDA is the only microcytic anaemia where total body iron stores are truly depleted. The other TAILS conditions — thalassaemia, ACD, lead poisoning, and sideroblastic anaemia — all have normal or raised iron stores.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sideroblastic anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In sideroblastic anaemia, iron accumulates in mitochondria of erythroblasts, forming ring sideroblasts. Serum iron and ferritin are elevated, not depleted.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the central insight: iron deficiency anaemia is a diagnosis of depleted stores. Thalassaemia, ACD, lead poisoning, and sideroblastic anaemia all have normal or raised iron — treating them with iron is at best useless, at worst harmful."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. IDA = depleted stores. The other four TAILS causes have normal or increased iron, making iron supplementation useless or harmful in those conditions."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
