          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient has microcytic anaemia with MCV 63 fL, RBC count 5.6 × 10¹²/L, and a n</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient has microcytic anaemia with MCV 63 fL, RBC count 5.6 × 10¹²/L, and a normal RDW. Ferritin and TIBC are both within normal range. Which diagnosis does this pattern most strongly suggest?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Iron deficiency anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In IDA, the RBC count is low (not raised), RDW is high (anisocytosis from uneven iron supply), ferritin is low, and TIBC is elevated. This patient's normal ferritin, normal TIBC, and high RBC count rule out IDA.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anaemia of chronic disease</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ACD typically shows normal or only mildly low MCV, a high ferritin, and a low TIBC — reflecting iron trapped by hepcidin. An MCV of 63 with a raised RBC count does not fit this pattern.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Beta-thalassaemia trait</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Beta-thalassaemia trait produces a very low MCV (often below 65 fL) with a normal or raised RBC count, a normal RDW (isocytic microcytes — all cells shrink uniformly), and normal iron studies. HbA2 on HPLC will be elevated to confirm.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sideroblastic anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sideroblastic anaemia shows a dimorphic smear with very high RDW, elevated serum iron and ferritin, and ring sideroblasts on marrow. This patient's normal RDW and normal ferritin do not fit.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Use the comparison table: IDA = low RBC count, high RDW, low ferritin, high TIBC. Beta-thalassaemia trait = raised RBC count, normal RDW, normal iron studies, very low MCV. This patient's raised RBC count with normal RDW and normal iron studies points to thalassaemia trait."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Very low MCV + raised RBC count + normal RDW + normal iron studies is the classic pattern of beta-thalassaemia trait — confirmed by raised HbA2 on HPLC."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
